        >
          {
            Object.entries(BUBBLES).map(
              ([name, { fill, edge, ink, hold, instant, dashed }]) => (
                <li>
                  <div
                    class:list={[
                      bubbleFont,
                      "relative rounded-xl border-2 px-4 py-3 text-[clamp(15px,1.15vw,19px)]/[1.35]",
                    ]}
                    style={`background:${fill};border-color:${edge};color:${ink};border-style:${dashed ? "dashed" : "solid"}`}
                  >
                    <p class="font-bold">{name}</p>
                    <p>
                      {instant ? "instant" : "staged"},{" "}
                      {(hold / 1000).toFixed(1)}s hold
                    </p>
                    <svg
                      aria-hidden="true"
                      viewBox="0 0 24 14"
                      class="absolute top-full left-6 -mt-0.5 h-3.5 w-6"
                    >
                      <path
                        d="M1 0 L12 12 L23 0"
                        fill={fill}
                        stroke={edge}
                        stroke-width="2"
                        stroke-dasharray={dashed ? "5 3" : undefined}
                      />
                    </svg>
                  </div>
                </li>
              ),
            )
          }
        </ul>
        <div class:list={[prose, "mt-[clamp(32px,4vw,64px)]"]}>
          <p>
            The voice is optional, and it’s my favourite small detail in the
            repo. It ranks your installed Windows voices and goes looking by
            name for Microsoft Sam, Mike, Mary, TruVoice, and yes, Bonzi, before
            falling back to something bright.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class:list={[grid, sectionGap]}>
        <SectionHeading {...oneInstaller} />
        <div class:list={[prose, headingGap]}>
          <p>
            On the release side, a version bump on{" "}
            <code class={code}>main</code> runs the packaging tests, builds an Inno
            Setup installer, smoke-tests it, and publishes with curated notes. The
            installer is about 35 MB. It then downloads the 5.7 GB model and the llama.cpp
            runtime during setup, which keeps the release to a single file.
          </p>
          <p>
            That’s part of the research too. It’s easy to overestimate a local
            LLM in a hand-tuned dev environment. Once you package the app and
            make the install story explicit, you stop thinking about impressive
            one-off outputs and start thinking about repeatable behavior. The
            interesting question was never whether a local model could say
            something entertaining once. It was whether it could carry a whole
            product loop: startup, onboarding, memory, idle presence,
            constrained actions, and visual feedback.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="bg-charcoal text-paper">
