        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="relative paper">
      <section
        data-remark="138 tests, and not one of them laughed."
        data-remark-style="joke"
        class:list={[
          grid,
          "pt-(--section-space) pb-[calc(var(--section-space)+clamp(64px,8vw,96px))]",
        ]}
      >
        <SectionHeading {...whatIdKeep} />
        <div class:list={[prose, headingGap]}>
          <p>
            The 138 tests aren’t there to chase coverage. They sit on the
            contracts that matter: controller behavior, output parsing,
            packaging helpers, profile persistence, animation rules, and
            presentation coordination. Those are the places where state, model
            output, and UI run into each other, so that’s where the risk is.
          </p>
          <p>
            So here’s my answer to the question that started this. Local LLMs
            are already good enough to power a narrow, personality-rich desktop
            companion, if the rest of the system is designed carefully around
            them. They aren’t good enough to trust blindly, and they don’t
            become good product components by accident. Given structure,
            guardrails, and a visible role in the UI, they’re a lot more useful
            than a novelty demo.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dipsy is also one of the clearest looks I’ve had at the gap between
            LLM capability and product capability. It works best when that gap
            is acknowledged instead of hidden. The model contributes
            interpretation, tone, and structured intent. The app contributes
            state, validation, timing, rendering, and safety. That division of
            labor is the main thing I’d carry into any future local-first
            assistant.
          </p>
          <p>
            It’s the kind of software I like building: playful on the surface,
            disciplined underneath, and opinionated about where the real
            complexity belongs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The project is on pause now, left runnable, packaged, and
            release-ready. If you’re on Windows, 1.0.0 is still there. It isn’t
            code signed, and it’ll want 5.7 GB for the model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p
          class:list={[
            mono,
            "col-span-2 col-start-2 mt-[clamp(32px,4vw,64px)] pl-(--col-inset) phone:pl-0",
          ]}
        >
